- op: discuss  # ecfe
  at: '2026-09-16T17:31:10Z'  # ecfe
  by: agent  # ecfe
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ecfe
  clock: 5  # ecfe
  nonce: ecfe  # ecfe
  message: "SATISFIED on positive evidence from two Rust corpora, 2026-09-16 at dev 1d38136c33.\n\nTHE STATEMENT: \"Every emitted symbol id has exactly one span slot, so the\nADR-0036 anchored parse has a unique correct answer for its path and name slots.\"\n\nMEASURED, both roots named because this repository has two corpus roots at\ndifferent commits:\n\n  root                                    .rs files  rust ids  qualified  double-span\n  ~/whole_bunch_of_repos/aardvark-dns     16         394       133        0\n  ~/whole_bunch_of_repos/ripgrep          100        5092      430        0\n\n\"qualified\" is the filed shape class -- external ids carrying a type- or\nmodule-qualified callee, which is where the double span came from. It is well\nexercised on both roots, so this is a clean read rather than an audit over a\nmissing input.\n\nTWO OF THE FOUR FILED EXEMPLARS ARE PRESENT IN ripgrep AND CONFORMANT:\n\n  filed  rust:external:0-0:Vec:0-0:with_capacity:external_symbol\n  tip    rust:external:0-0:Vec..with_capacity:external_symbol\n\n  filed  rust:external:0-0:Stdio:0-0:null:external_symbol\n  tip    rust:external:0-0:std..process..Stdio..null:external_symbol\n\nTHE MECHANISM, so this is not a coincidence of sampling. The path slot keeps\n\"::\" -- ADR-0036 Ruling 1's one colon-tolerant slot -- while the NAME slot\nnormalizes \"::\" to \"..\". One id shows both rules at once:\n\n  rust:std::process:0-0:Stdio..piped:external_symbol\n       ^^^^^^^^^^^^ path keeps ::      ^^^^^^^^^^^^ name uses ..\n\nBecause the name slot can no longer contain \"::\", it can no longer contain a\nsecond span token, so the double-span form is structurally unconstructible\nrather than merely absent from a sample. The conformant contrast form the item\ncites is also emitted for the very calls that were filed as broken:\naardvark-dns produces rust:std::fs:0-0:read_dir:external_symbol.\n\nWHAT I COULD NOT DO. The filed corpora, rage and just, are in neither corpus\nroot, so the original \"483 double-span ids across rage + just\" could not be\nre-run as filed. The substitutes were chosen for shape coverage, not\nconvenience, and the exemplars above are the item's own.\n\nSEPARATE FINDING, NOT A RESIDUAL OF THIS ROW, recorded because it is only\nvisible on a Rust-heavy corpus and belongs to INV-dulah's by-design \"::\" limb:\non ripgrep, 526 of 5091 rust node ids (10.3%) carry more than four colons --\n166 at 6, 258 at 8, 84 at 10, 13 at 12, 3 at 14, 2 at 16. On the hypergumbo\nself-analysis the same limb reads 2 of 46399 (0.004%), because this tree has\nalmost no Rust. The exemption is declared and is not reopened here, but a\nconsumer parsing ids positionally is dealing with 10% of ids on such a repo,\nnot a rounding error. Measure that limb on a corpus where the language is\nactually present."  # ecfe
